<p>
  A kitchen island is the single most-requested feature in Plano kitchen remodels. Whether it is seating for the family, a second prep area, or a sink and dishwasher tucked out of sight, an island changes how a kitchen works — and how much it costs.
</p>

<h2>What an island adds to a Plano kitchen</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Seating and gathering</strong> — an island with an overhang becomes the natural spot for breakfast and homework, especially in open floor plans in West Plano and Legacy.</li>
  <li><strong>Storage</strong> — deep drawers, hidden trash pull-outs, and appliance garages make the island the most functional part of the kitchen.</li>
  <li><strong>Prep space</strong> — a dedicated prep zone keeps the main counters clear and puts the work triangle closer together.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Island with plumbing? That changes the cost</h2>
<p>
  An island is most affordable when it is simple — just countertop, cabinetry, and maybe a couple of outlets. The moment you add a sink, a dishwasher, or an appliance, you are adding plumbing and electrical rough-in, which is the biggest cost driver. In Plano, adding or relocating plumbing also typically means a permit.
</p>

<h2>Space and layout considerations</h2>
<p>
  The most common mistake is an island that is too big for the room. You generally want at least 36–42 inches of clearance around the island so doors and drawers open freely and two people can pass. For most Plano homes, an island that seats two to four with storage below hits the sweet spot.
</p>

<h2>Cost context</h2>
<p>
  An island is almost always part of a full kitchen remodel rather than a standalone addition, and those projects typically run <strong>$40,000–$100,000+</strong> depending on cabinetry, countertops, appliances, and layout changes. Islands with seating, outlets, and built-in storage are standard in our custom kitchen remodels. See our <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-kitchen-remodel-cost-in-plano/">kitchen remodel cost guide</a> and the full <a href="/cost-guides/">kitchen &amp; bathroom cost guides</a> for realistic ranges.
</p>

<h2>Island countertop choices</h2>
<p>
  Because the island is the visual centerpiece, many Plano homeowners choose a slightly different countertop there — a waterfall edge, a contrasting quartz, or a butcher block prep section. Our <a href="/blog/quartz-vs-granite-countertops-plano-kitchens/">quartz vs. granite guide</a> can help you compare the most popular options.
</p>

